WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.312
Merge sort needs extra arrays to merge into.

2
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.066
Quicksort sorts inside the

3
00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.340
array it is given.

4
00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.920
It is usually very fast.

5
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.020
But feed it a list that is already sorted

6
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.106
five hundred items

7
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.145
and it makes more than a hundred and

8
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.370
twenty thousand comparisons.

9
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.610
Shuffle the same list first

10
00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.530
and it needs about five thousand.

11
00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:27.070
Same algorithm, same items

12
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.560
about twenty-five times less work.

13
00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.300
Let's see why

14
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.110
and how one random choice fixes it.

15
00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.490
This is quicksort.

16
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.713
We'll see how partitioning works

17
00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.257
why the balance of the split

18
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:41.670
decides the cost

19
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.360
and why a random pivot protects us.

20
00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.110
Quicksort picks one item, called the pivot.

21
00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.860
Here we take the last one.

22
00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.334
Partitioning rearranges the list

23
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.628
so that everything smaller than

24
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.420
the pivot is on its left

25
00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.460
everything else is on its right

26
00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.930
and the pivot sits between them.

27
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.890
Sweep through the list with an index j.

28
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.170
Keep a boundary i.

29
00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.284
Everything before i is smaller

30
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:09.360
than the pivot.

31
00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.000
When item j is smaller than the pivot

32
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.780
swap it to the boundary

33
00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.470
and move the boundary on.

34
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.970
Seven is not smaller than five, so it stays.

35
00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:22.370
Two is smaller: swap it to the boundary.

36
00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:23.810
Nine stays.

37
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.970
Four is smaller: swap.

38
00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:28.530
Three is smaller: swap.

39
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.020
Eight stays.

40
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:31.580
After the sweep

41
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.339
swap the pivot into the boundary.

42
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.769
Now the pivot is exactly where it

43
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.200
belongs in the sorted list

44
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.530
and it never moves again.

45
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.085
Partitioning n items takes

46
00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.070
n minus one comparisons

47
00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:47.280
one for each item against the pivot.

48
00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.290
Now the recursion.

49
00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.550
The pivot is in place.

50
00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.210
Sort the left part, and sort the right part

51
00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.420
the same way.

52
00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.060
There is nothing to merge afterwards.

53
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.100
When the recursion finishes

54
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.050
the whole array is sorted.

55
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.234
Compare with merge sort

56
00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:05.635
merge sort does its work

57
00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.350
after the recursion

58
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:08.960
quicksort does it before.

59
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.110
How much does it cost?

60
00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:14.460
It depends on how the pivot splits the list.

61
00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.220
If the pivot lands near the middle

62
00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.920
we get two halves.

63
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.660
Partitioning costs n

64
00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.420
so T of n is two T of n over two, plus n.

65
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.090
You know this one: n log n.

66
00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.149
Every level of the recursion

67
00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:31.850
tree costs about n

68
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.600
and there are about log n levels.

69
00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:36.590
Pause and predict.

70
00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.386
The list is already sorted

71
00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.051
and we always take the last

72
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.450
item as the pivot.

73
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.260
How does the split look?

74
00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.120
The last item is the biggest one.

75
00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.540
Everything else is smaller

76
00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.240
so it all goes to the left.

77
00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.730
The right part is empty.

78
00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:52.669
One item is placed

79
00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.427
and we are left with a list that

80
00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.260
is just one item shorter.

81
00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:58.240
And it is sorted again

82
00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.950
so the same thing happens.

83
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.890
T of n equals T of n minus one

84
00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.993
plus n minus one.

85
00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:07.985
Each level of the chain costs

86
00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.420
one less than the one above.

87
00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:14.400
Unroll it: n minus one, plus n minus two

88
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.320
and so on.

89
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.340
That is n times n minus one over two.

90
00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.410
The same number as comparing every pair.

91
00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.810
For five hundred items

92
00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.070
a hundred twenty-four thousand

93
00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.690
seven hundred fifty comparisons.

94
00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.880
The easiest-looking input is the worst one.

95
00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.230
The fix is to choose the pivot at random.

96
00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.150
Pick a random item and swap it to the end

97
00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.380
and then partition as before.

98
00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:42.295
Now no input is bad by design.

99
00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.030
About half of the time the pivot lands

100
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.800
in the middle half of the list

101
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.770
and that already guarantees a decent split.

102
00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.612
On average the cost is about one

103
00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.830
point four n log n for large n

104
00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.090
whatever the input.

105
00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.250
A bad split can still happen

106
00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.000
but a long run of them is very unlikely.

107
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.180
Double the size, and watch what happens.

108
00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.180
With the last item as the pivot

109
00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:09.400
on sorted input

110
00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.680
the comparisons go up four times each time

111
00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.960
about five thousand, twenty thousand

112
00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.340
eighty thousand

113
00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.930
three hundred twenty thousand.

114
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.710
On random input

115
00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:23.070
they go up a little more than two times

116
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.840
the n log n pattern.

117
00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.010
Two cautions.

118
00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:30.620
First, our partition puts only strictly

119
00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:32.670
smaller items on the left.

120
00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.930
If every item is equal, nothing goes left

121
00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.628
and we get the worst case again.

122
00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.800
Real libraries use a three-way

123
00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.180
partition for that.

124
00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.780
Second, the recursion depth.

125
00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:47.060
In the bad case it is n

126
00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:48.547
and the stack can overflow.

127
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.339
A standard trick is to recurse on the smaller

128
00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.680
part and loop on the larger one

129
00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.890
which keeps the depth at log n.

130
00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.691
Quicksort works in place and is

131
00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.690
often fastest in practice.

132
00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.470
But unlike merge sort, it is not stable

133
00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:06.000
and its worst case is real.

134
00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:07.590
To sum up.

135
00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.450
Partition places the pivot

136
00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.190
then recurse on both sides.

137
00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.070
A balanced split gives n log n

138
00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.538
a lopsided split gives n squared

139
00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:18.901
and a random pivot makes lopsided

140
00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.180
splits unlikely.

141
00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:23.580
Next chapter: trees as data structures

142
00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.090
where this idea of balance comes back.

143
00:05:26.717 --> 00:05:29.007
Let's write quicksort in Java

144
00:05:29.007 --> 00:05:31.277
and watch the worst case happen.

145
00:05:31.857 --> 00:05:35.047
The pivot is the last item of the array.

146
00:05:35.047 --> 00:05:37.407
The index i is the boundary

147
00:05:37.407 --> 00:05:41.047
and the index j sweeps from lo up to

148
00:05:41.047 --> 00:05:43.267
but not including, hi.

149
00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:46.087
Each comparison is counted.

150
00:05:46.087 --> 00:05:48.647
When an item is smaller than the pivot

151
00:05:48.647 --> 00:05:50.627
we swap it to the boundary

152
00:05:50.627 --> 00:05:53.027
using a small swap helper

153
00:05:53.027 --> 00:05:54.897
and move the boundary on.

154
00:05:55.247 --> 00:05:56.257
At the end

155
00:05:56.257 --> 00:05:58.517
swap the pivot into the boundary

156
00:05:58.517 --> 00:06:00.007
and return its index.

157
00:06:00.357 --> 00:06:02.607
The array is sorted in place.

158
00:06:02.607 --> 00:06:04.677
No new arrays are allocated.

159
00:06:05.257 --> 00:06:07.821
The public method takes just the array

160
00:06:07.887 --> 00:06:09.996
and calls the three-argument version with

161
00:06:10.047 --> 00:06:13.017
the bounds zero and length minus one.

162
00:06:13.367 --> 00:06:16.541
If lo is less than hi, partition

163
00:06:16.637 --> 00:06:18.957
then sort the part before the

164
00:06:19.037 --> 00:06:21.197
pivot and the part after it.

165
00:06:21.197 --> 00:06:23.567
The pivot is already in place.

166
00:06:24.147 --> 00:06:25.837
Now the experiment.

167
00:06:25.837 --> 00:06:28.244
With the last item as the pivot

168
00:06:28.317 --> 00:06:31.311
sorted input costs four thousand nine hundred

169
00:06:31.377 --> 00:06:33.482
fifty comparisons for a hundred items

170
00:06:33.537 --> 00:06:35.741
then about four times as much

171
00:06:35.817 --> 00:06:37.074
each time we double.

172
00:06:37.137 --> 00:06:39.266
For five hundred items it is a

173
00:06:39.337 --> 00:06:41.257
hundred twenty-four thousand

174
00:06:41.257 --> 00:06:42.347
seven hundred fifty.

175
00:06:42.697 --> 00:06:45.976
Random input costs eight thousand four hundred

176
00:06:46.047 --> 00:06:48.167
seventy-one for eight hundred items

177
00:06:48.167 --> 00:06:50.907
against three hundred nineteen thousand

178
00:06:50.907 --> 00:06:52.377
six hundred for sorted.

179
00:06:52.957 --> 00:06:55.798
The recursion also goes n levels

180
00:06:55.887 --> 00:06:57.547
deep on sorted input.

181
00:06:57.547 --> 00:07:00.207
With a long enough sorted array

182
00:07:00.207 --> 00:07:01.743
Java's stack overflows

183
00:07:01.807 --> 00:07:03.249
and the program ends with

184
00:07:03.307 --> 00:07:04.997
a StackOverflowError.

185
00:07:05.347 --> 00:07:07.395
The exact size depends on

186
00:07:07.477 --> 00:07:08.537
the thread's stack

187
00:07:08.537 --> 00:07:11.707
but the cause is always the lopsided split.

188
00:07:12.287 --> 00:07:14.757
The random version picks a random position

189
00:07:14.757 --> 00:07:16.437
between lo and hi

190
00:07:16.437 --> 00:07:18.497
and swaps that item to the end.

191
00:07:18.497 --> 00:07:20.837
The bound of nextInt is exclusive

192
00:07:20.837 --> 00:07:24.407
so we ask for hi minus lo plus one.

193
00:07:24.757 --> 00:07:27.387
The Random object has a fixed seed

194
00:07:27.387 --> 00:07:30.037
so the numbers on screen can be reproduced.

195
00:07:30.387 --> 00:07:33.093
On the sorted array of five hundred items

196
00:07:33.157 --> 00:07:34.714
it needs four thousand seven hundred

197
00:07:34.757 --> 00:07:36.317
forty-four comparisons

198
00:07:36.317 --> 00:07:38.977
not a hundred twenty-four thousand

199
00:07:38.977 --> 00:07:40.107
seven hundred fifty.

200
00:07:40.687 --> 00:07:42.714
Java's sort for primitive arrays

201
00:07:42.777 --> 00:07:45.477
is a quicksort variant, with two pivots

202
00:07:45.477 --> 00:07:47.937
and with insertion sort for small parts

203
00:07:47.937 --> 00:07:50.837
so it is far from our simple version.

204
00:07:50.837 --> 00:07:53.717
For objects, Java uses TimSort

205
00:07:53.717 --> 00:07:54.927
which is stable.

206
00:07:55.507 --> 00:07:58.497
So in Java: partition in place

207
00:07:58.497 --> 00:08:00.644
recurse on both sides

208
00:08:00.737 --> 00:08:03.049
and a random pivot turns the worst

209
00:08:03.117 --> 00:08:06.027
case from certain into unlikely.

210
00:08:06.377 --> 00:08:08.747
That ends our tour of recursion.

211
00:08:08.747 --> 00:08:11.017
Next chapter: trees.

